1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,171
This is CampusGear, a database application developed for the Track One project in Storing and Retrieving Data.

2
00:00:07,171 --> 00:00:17,294
The problem is straightforward: shared equipment needs a consistent reservation record, and an item must not be promised to two people for overlapping times.

3
00:00:17,294 --> 00:00:21,091
All members and equipment in this demonstration are synthetic.

4
00:00:21,091 --> 00:00:25,309
This is a local prototype, not an operational university service.

5
00:00:25,309 --> 00:00:31,636
The application runs as one Python process with a SQLite database and a browser interface.

6
00:00:31,636 --> 00:00:39,650
We will demonstrate the catalogue, create a reservation, reject a conflict, complete a loan, and inspect the actual database.

7
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:44,712
We will also show measured query performance rather than a simulated dashboard.

8
00:00:44,712 --> 00:00:48,898
The equipment catalogue is loaded from the backend API.

9
00:00:48,898 --> 00:00:52,620
Search and category filters issue real database queries.

10
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:57,737
Each physical item has its own identifier and unique asset tag.

11
00:00:57,737 --> 00:01:04,714
Category names are not copied into every reservation: the interface obtains them through relational joins.

12
00:01:04,714 --> 00:01:12,622
Members also belong to departments, and a reservation references a member and an item using foreign keys.

13
00:01:12,622 --> 00:01:16,343
This keeps descriptive information separate from transaction records.

14
00:01:16,343 --> 00:01:20,530
Notice the distinction between equipment condition and reservation status.

15
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:27,972
An item marked available is operational; it does not mean every future time slot is free.

16
00:01:27,972 --> 00:01:32,159
Maintenance and retired equipment should not accept new reservations.

17
00:01:32,159 --> 00:01:35,880
The member selector is for demonstration, not authentication.

18
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:43,414
Now we select an available item and a synthetic member, enter a purpose, and choose a future interval.

19
00:01:43,414 --> 00:01:46,344
The interface labels the input as UTC.

20
00:01:46,344 --> 00:01:57,226
The server requires a canonical timestamp including the final Z, validates that it is a real calendar time, and checks that the start precedes the end.

21
00:01:57,226 --> 00:02:00,575
On submission, the browser sends a JSON request.

22
00:02:00,575 --> 00:02:06,016
The backend starts a write transaction and inserts a reservation with confirmed status.

23
00:02:06,016 --> 00:02:12,294
Database constraints validate the references and values, and a trigger checks for conflicting active reservations.

24
00:02:12,294 --> 00:02:16,061
The audit event is recorded with the same transaction.

25
00:02:16,061 --> 00:02:20,665
Only after a successful commit does the interface refresh the list.

26
00:02:20,665 --> 00:02:24,432
This is the full browser, server, and database path.

27
00:02:24,432 --> 00:02:30,552
We now attempt a second reservation for the same equipment and the same time interval.

28
00:02:30,552 --> 00:02:35,040
The expected result is a conflict, not a second confirmed booking.

29
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:40,752
This rule is protected in the database rather than only in the web form.

30
00:02:40,752 --> 00:02:50,136
Two intervals overlap when the existing start is earlier than the proposed end and the existing end is later than the proposed start.

31
00:02:50,136 --> 00:02:52,992
The strict comparisons implement half open intervals.

32
00:02:52,992 --> 00:02:57,072
Therefore, a reservation beginning exactly when another ends is allowed.

33
00:02:57,072 --> 00:02:59,112
We demonstrate that boundary separately.

34
00:02:59,112 --> 00:03:03,192
Cancelled and returned reservations do not block new time slots.

35
00:03:03,192 --> 00:03:11,352
This policy is a deliberate prototype rule, and concurrent attempts must still obey it when separate database connections are used.

36
00:03:11,352 --> 00:03:14,313
Next we check out the confirmed reservation.

37
00:03:14,313 --> 00:03:21,080
The application creates a loan record and moves the reservation to checked out within one transaction.

38
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:24,463
A reservation can have at most one loan.

39
00:03:24,463 --> 00:03:32,499
The application also prevents two open loans for the same physical item, including bookings with otherwise separate planned intervals.

40
00:03:32,499 --> 00:03:38,844
For demonstration, early checkout is permitted; a production system would need an approved pickup window.

41
00:03:38,844 --> 00:03:44,765
Returning the equipment records an actual return time and moves the reservation to returned.

42
00:03:44,765 --> 00:03:48,994
That is different from simply reaching the planned end time.

43
00:03:48,994 --> 00:03:54,916
A confirmed reservation may be cancelled, while a checked out item must be returned.

44
00:03:54,916 --> 00:03:59,568
Invalid repeated actions are rejected instead of silently changing historical records.

45
00:03:59,568 --> 00:04:04,525
The database inspector reads table and index definitions from SQLite itself.

46
00:04:04,525 --> 00:04:09,031
It is not a hard coded picture of the schema.

47
00:04:09,031 --> 00:04:14,889
The main relations cover departments, members, categories, equipment, reservations, loans, and audit events.

48
00:04:14,889 --> 00:04:18,494
Primary keys identify rows; foreign keys link them.

49
00:04:18,494 --> 00:04:23,902
Unique constraints protect asset tags and the one loan per reservation rule.

50
00:04:23,902 --> 00:04:25,704
Check constraints restrict domains.

51
00:04:25,704 --> 00:04:32,013
Triggers handle the cross row overlap rule that an ordinary row check cannot express.

52
00:04:32,013 --> 00:04:36,519
The audit view exposes the effects of our actual actions.

53
00:04:36,519 --> 00:04:43,729
This event log helps explain what happened, but it is not a tamper proof security ledger.

54
00:04:43,729 --> 00:04:51,840
The application deliberately separates planned reservation times from actual loan timestamps and calculates overdue loans from those facts.

55
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:53,699
Evaluation has two parts.

56
00:04:53,699 --> 00:05:04,387
Correctness tests exercise successful operations and rejected boundary cases, including overlap protection, adjacent intervals, state transitions, malformed timestamps, foreign keys, and concurrent requests.

57
00:05:04,387 --> 00:05:08,104
The included test output records the real run.

58
00:05:08,104 --> 00:05:16,469
The benchmark generates deterministic synthetic reservations at three scales: one thousand, ten thousand, and one hundred thousand rows.

59
00:05:16,469 --> 00:05:26,692
It measures the same overlap query without and with the target index, confirms equal results, and records repeated timings and query plans.

60
00:05:26,692 --> 00:05:29,480
The report contains the actual measurements.

61
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:38,309
These are warm cache query microbenchmarks on one machine, not network load tests or proof of campus wide scalability.

62
00:05:38,309 --> 00:05:45,744
Index maintenance and write costs remain relevant, and the speedup applies only to the measured workload.

63
00:05:45,744 --> 00:05:52,241
The main result is a reproducible local application whose database protects a meaningful workflow.

64
00:05:52,241 --> 00:05:55,025
It is intentionally not production ready.

65
00:05:55,025 --> 00:06:03,843
There is no real login, student versus administrator authorization, single sign on, email notification, payment handling, or deployment hardening.

66
00:06:03,843 --> 00:06:14,516
Python's built in HTTP server is used to keep the course demonstration dependency free, and the service binds only to the local machine.

67
00:06:14,516 --> 00:06:22,405
The source package includes the schema, backend, frontend, test suite, data generation, benchmark code, and running instructions.

68
00:06:22,405 --> 00:06:27,510
The report documents the architecture, design decisions, measured results, and limitations.

69
00:06:27,510 --> 00:06:35,863
Before submission, the group must confirm its real members and contributions and provide accessible repository and video links.

70
00:06:35,863 --> 00:06:40,968
AI assistance is disclosed rather than attributed to fictional student work.
